Important Thing to Maintaining Youthful Skin."

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.880
Let's do this.

00:02:58.233 --> 00:03:00.240
Considered the single most
important practice

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.160
for maintaining youthful skin:

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.000
the daily application
of sunscreen

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.680
and taking
other protective measures,

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.440
like wearing a hat.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.200
All the other things
you can do for your skin

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.640
pale in comparison,

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.520
especially for those
with pale skin.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:16.000
Up to 90 percent
of visible facial aging

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.520
among people
with lighter skin tones

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.400
is due to exposure to sunlight.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.720
Those with darker skin
are relatively protected

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.040
due to their built-in
melanin sunscreen,

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.480
but they're still affected.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.360
Sun damage in darker skin
tends to be less about wrinkles

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.440
and more
about pigmentation issues,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.760
such as uneven skin tone,
dark patches,

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.080
and small dark bumps
on the face.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.920
Either way,
dermatologists now agree

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.680
that there is
nothing more important

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:41.280
to slow the signs of aging

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.560
than to protect your skin
from the sun.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.680
To illustrate, here's
a dramatic photo of a trucker

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.680
who spent decades
getting more sun

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:50.720
on the left side of his face

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.880
through
his driver's side window,

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.400
reminiscent of a Batman villain.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.480
Factors like sun exposure
and smoking

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.200
can make us look
up to 11 years older.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.440
Compare that to extensive
cosmetic surgeryâ€”

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.640
a face lift and a neck lift,
and removing excess skin

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.800
from both the upper
and lower eyelids,

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:10.880
and a forehead lift,
which combined,

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.120
can make us look
about eight years younger.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.280
So, a healthy lifestyle
may work even better.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.200
Protecting your skin
from the sun

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.120
should be a lifelong endeavor.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.960
This can involve
applying sunscreen,

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.000
wearing sun-protective clothing,
hats, and sunglasses,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.360
and avoiding direct sunlight

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.760
during the peak hours
of 10:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.880
and instead
seeking shady, covered areas.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.320
Sunbathing is frowned upon,

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.160
even with sunscreens
like zinc oxide

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.960
or titanium dioxide that offer
broad-spectrum protection

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.520
against both UVA
and UVB rays.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.680
We now know
that other wavelengths,

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:44.960
such as near infrared,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.640
that are not covered
by sunscreens

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.960
also contribute to skin aging.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.120
Men and women
who use tanning beds

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.520
appear significantly older
than those who don't,

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.920
and white women who sunbathe
appear years older

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:56.960
than they actually are,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.840
similar to what is seen
with smoking.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.440
UVA rays are primarily
responsible for skin aging,

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:06.400
whereas UVB are the rays
that cause sunburn,

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.440
but a broad-spectrum sunscreen
covering both is recommended

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.040
since both types of UV
contribute to cancer risk.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:14.320
To prevent skin cancer,

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.400
the American Academy
of Dermatology recommends

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.520
sunscreen with an SPF of 30
or higher,

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:23.680
but an SPF even as low as 15
can help prevent skin aging.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:25.200
How do we know?

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.840
Because it's been
put to the test.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:30.960
Despite the widespread belief

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:33.520
that the use of sunscreen
would prevent skin aging,

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:36.480
all we had was data
on hairless mice.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.320
That is, until ten years ago.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:41.760
Nine hundred adults
were randomized

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.680
either to years
of daily sunscreen use

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.200
or to continue with
their own discretionary use.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.920
(It was considered unethical
to withhold protection

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.400
by giving people
placebo sunscreen.)

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.080
In the end, 77 percent

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:55.840
in the recommended
daily sunscreen group

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:56.800
were applying sunscreen

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:58.880
at least three to four days
per week

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:00.160
compared with only 33 percent

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.160
in the discretionary use group.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:03.760
Would that be
enough of a difference

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.440
to make a difference?

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:08.480
Yes, there was
significantly less skin aging

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.560
in the instructed
daily use group.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:11.600
In fact, they suffered

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.520
no detectable increase
in skin aging

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.360
over the four-and-a-half-year
study.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.760
The researchers concluded
"Regular sunscreen use

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.360
retards skin aging in healthy,
middle-aged men and women."

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.880
Okay, so to reiterate, there's nothing&nbsp;
more important to slow the signs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:36.240
aging than to protect your skin from the sun.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.960
This involves avoiding direct sunlight&nbsp;
since they're skin aging wavelengths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:46.800
not covered by sunscreens and wearing&nbsp;
a daily SPF 30 plus sunscreen proven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.800
to slow aging in a randomized controlled trial.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:58.720
Okay, but what if you care less about how&nbsp;
you look and more about avoiding cancer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:05.680
Has sunscreen been similarly proven to prevent&nbsp;
skin cancer? Let's find out in my next video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.320
"Does sunscreen really prevent skin cancer?".

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:12.960
Let's find out.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.200
Decades ago,
studies started bubbling up,

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.120
reporting that not only
was the use of sunscreen

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:23.120
not found to be protective
against skin cancer,

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.880
but it was associated with
significantly elevated risk.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:30.080
But subsequent studies offered
the obvious explanation

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:31.520
of this paradox.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:35.440
People who use sunscreen are
more likely to be sunbathers.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.920
That's like finding those
who use motorcycle helmets

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:39.920
are more likely to die
on the highway.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.320
It's not that sunscreen
and helmets aren't protective;

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:43.520
it's just
that they're associated

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.400
with engagement
in risky activities.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:50.640
But you don't know,
until you put it to the test.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.200
As I noted in my last video,
randomized controlled trials

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:55.040
have shown that regular use
of sunscreen

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:57.600
can arrest visible signs
of skin aging,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.440
including
biopsy-proven reductions

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:01.520
in UV-related skin damage.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:03.040
But are there
interventional trials

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:06.000
proving sunscreen
can prevent cancer?

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.360
Yes.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:08.720
Organ transplant recipients

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:10.800
are highly susceptible
to skin cancers

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.120
because they have to be given
immune suppressants

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.120
to prevent organ rejection.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.280
A group of 120 organ recipients

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:20.000
were equally informed
about sun protection measures,

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.760
but half were given free

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:23.920
broad spectrum
SPF 50+ sunscreen

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:26.000
for daily application
to their head, neck,

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.520
forearms, and hands.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:30.800
After two years, there were
nine new basal carcinomas

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:32.320
in the control group
versus only two

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:33.600
in the sunscreen group.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.480
That sounds good, but it may
have just been a fluke.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:39.040
In contrast, there was
a highly significant difference

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:40.160
in the proportion of patients

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:42.800
who got new invasive
squamous cell carcinomas.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:44.320
Eight new cases
in the control group

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:47.360
versus zero
in the sunscreen group.

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.480
But wait until you hear
about what happened

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:52.160
to their actinic keratoses,

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:56.320
the precancerous growths
that can turn into skin cancer.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:58.800
They all started out
covered in them,

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.680
a total of 191 detected
in each group.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:03.200
In the subsequent 24 months,

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.160
the control group
developed 82 new ones.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:09.200
How many did
the sunscreen group develop?

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.600
Negative 102.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.840
More than 100
precancerous growths

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:17.040
in the sunscreen group
spontaneously regressed

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.880
and vanished completely
(compared to 82 new growths

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:22.080
developing in the control group

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:24.000
without a single one
disappearing).

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:27.120
Their bodies, even in
their immunocompromised state,

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.920
could heal itself
once it just stopped

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:33.440
being bombarded with
so many cancer-causing rays.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:34.880
Other randomized
controlled trials

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:36.880
have shown similar findings,

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:39.440
but generalizability
is limited.

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:41.920
The studies all used
high-risk populations,

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:44.080
for example, those living
in subtropical climates,

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:47.200
or with a personal history
of precancerous lesions.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.480
So, cancer prevention efficacy
in the general population

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:52.560
would be expected to be less.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:55.600
On the other hand, the studies
could also have underestimated

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.240
the impact since they were
relatively short-term

00:09:58.240 --> 00:09:59.360
(not exceeding four years)

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:03.040
and, ethically, had to allow
the control group participants

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:04.880
to slather on
their own sunscreen,

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:07.760
which would dilute
the difference between groups.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:09.440
For maximum effectiveness,

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:11.920
sunscreen needs to be
applied properly,

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:14.800
which apparently rarely happens
in the real world.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.680
In a study of nearly 5,000
skiers and snowboarders,

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:18.800
almost no one,

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.200
only about 4 percent
were fully compliant

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:22.880
with sunscreen recommendations.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:25.040
First, there's the amount.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:26.960
The FDA standard
is two milligrams

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.800
per square centimeter.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:30.320
What does that mean?

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:32.400
Use the teaspoon rule:

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:35.200
one teaspoon for your face,
head and neck,

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:36.880
one for the front
of your torso,

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:39.120
another for your back,
one teaspoon for each arm

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:40.960
and two teaspoons for each leg.

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.880
That's a total
of nine teaspoons.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:44.320
That's about
the total volume equivalent

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:49.280
of a golf ball or shot glass,
to help you visualize it.

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:51.360
Unfortunately,
the average sunscreen user

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:54.240
may only apply a quarter
of the recommended amount.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:57.040
This is why high SPF sunscreens
are often recommended,

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:59.200
like 50+ SPF.

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:02.560
Although the FDA recommends
a minimum SPF of only 15

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:04.320
to prevent skin cancer,

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:06.400
under normal
consumer conditions,

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.280
even an SPF of 50
may effectively give you

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:10.960
only an SPF of 12.5,

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.840
because most people
don't put on enough.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:14.960
Randomized double-blind,

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:17.280
head-to-head,
split-face experiments,

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:19.440
where you're randomized
to apply one sunscreen

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:20.400
to one side of your face

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:22.400
and another sunscreen
to the other side,

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:24.480
show that in real life use,

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:28.320
SPF 100+ sunscreen works
significantly better

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:30.800
than SPF 50+ sunscreen.

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:32.640
More than 50 percent
of the participants

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.600
were sunburned more
on their SPF 50+ side

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:37.680
compared to only
about 5 percent

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.400
on their SPF 100+ side.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:42.880
Now, a potential downside
of higher SPF sunscreens

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:45.840
is that they could provide
a false sense of security.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:48.640
Those randomized
to an SPF 30 sunscreen

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:50.960
ended up spending
more cumulative time in the sun

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:53.440
than those receiving
an SPF 10 sunscreen,

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:56.800
as much as five times longer
in some cases.

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:57.680
What else?

00:11:57.680 --> 00:11:59.760
Well, people tend to remember
to use sunscreen

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:01.280
on a sunny day at the beach,

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:03.760
but sun protection is needed
even on cloudy days,

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:05.440
since the UV rays
are not dampened

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:07.440
as much as visible light.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:10.480
There's even a phenomenon
known as "cloud enhancement"

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:12.480
where overcast skies
can sometimes result

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:14.880
in even more UV reaching
the Earth's surface

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:16.640
compared to clear skies.

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:19.360
Sunscreen labels often suggest
waiting at least 15 minutes

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:21.760
after application
before going outdoors,

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:23.200
but when put to the test,

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:25.280
sunscreen was found
to start working immediately

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:28.080
with the full effect apparent
by minute ten.

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.720
However, if water resistance
is required,

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:31.760
it may be prudent to wait

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:34.240
the full 15 to 30 minutes
after application

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:36.320
before taking a dip.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:38.560
The terms "waterproof"
or "sweatproof"

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:40.480
appear to be
meaningless marketing,

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:42.240
as no difference
in retention was noted

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:45.200
between "waterproof" products
and those that were

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:47.280
merely labeled
"water-resistant."

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:48.400
Both were better than

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:50.240
non-water-resistant products,
though,

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:52.480
which lost nearly
all their protective effects

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:55.760
within 20 minutes
of water immersion.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:58.080
The suggestion that sunscreen
should be reapplied

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:00.400
every two to three hours
only applies under conditions

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:02.800
in which it is rinsed off
by water or sweat,

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:06.320
or rubbed off by friction
from clothing or sand.

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:09.840
Even after allowing sunscreen
to dry first for 20 minutes,

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:12.640
between 15 and 60 percent
of its protective effect

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.920
can be lost
after contact with sand.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:18.000
Otherwise, if the recommended
amount is applied

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:19.920
and the sunscreen layer
is not disturbed,

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:24.080
SPF can be maintained
for as long as eight hours.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:32.320
Oh, tons of content in that video.

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:39.840
I mean, I think the coolest thing was that&nbsp;
study showing the spontaneous regression of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:43.600
more than a hundred precancerous&nbsp;
growths in the sunscreen group.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:45.280
Vanishing completely, right?

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:51.160
Their bodies—even in that immunocompromised&nbsp;
state—could heal themselves when they just—

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:54.480
when we just—stopped bombarding them&nbsp;
with so many cancer-causing rays, right?

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:59.760
It reminds me of the famous Lifestyle&nbsp;
Heart Trial from Dean Ornish,

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:03.760
showing that you could, you know, open up&nbsp;
arteries without drugs, without surgery—

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:06.480
just by putting people on a&nbsp;
plant-based diet and lifestyle program.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:10.960
Suggesting that their bodies&nbsp;
wanted to be healthy all along,

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:17.040
and all we had to do was remove the fork we&nbsp;
were stabbing ourselves with three times a day,

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:24.640
and then the body could just heal naturally&nbsp;
once we take away the kind of noxious stimuli.

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:29.600
And so, in this case, it’s&nbsp;
the UV rays from the sun.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:34.400
Okay, so, you know—the teaspoon&nbsp;
rule, maybe go 50+ SPF.

00:14:34.400 --> 00:14:39.200
And then sunny days, cloudy days—that cloud&nbsp;
enhancement thing I never heard of, that’s wild.

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:44.880
But okay, the one thing I didn’t&nbsp;
cover: which sunscreen is best.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:46.880
And that’s what I’m going to be wrapping up with—

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:57.040
the best type of sunscreen to use.

00:14:57.040 --> 00:14:59.360
What kind of sunscreen
should you use?

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:01.440
Cream-based is preferable
to spray-on,

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:02.880
since the completeness
of application

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:04.960
is more readily visualized.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.400
To help with adequate coverage,

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:07.680
spray-on sunscreens should be

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:09.760
rubbed on immediately
after spraying.

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:12.240
Aerosolized sunscreens
are flammable

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:15.440
and can combust on skin
upon exposure to an open flame,

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:18.160
even after the sunscreen
has dried.

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:20.080
What's more,
the safety of breathing in

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:21.520
aerosolized sunscreen chemicals

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:24.080
has not been
adequately studied,

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:26.080
though frankly the same thing
could be said

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:28.560
about rubbing them
on your skin.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.640
The concerns
surrounding sunscreen safety

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:32.240
are three-fold:

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:34.480
increased
intentional sun exposure,

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:37.360
vitamin D deficiency,
and untoward effects

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:41.200
from systemic absorption
of sunscreen chemicals.

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:45.120
In the 1800s, we first learned
that UVB caused sunburns

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:47.360
and formulated sunscreens
to block it.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:48.800
Nearly a century later,
we learned

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:50.800
about the contribution of UVA.

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:52.480
Now, a full century later,

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:55.200
we are realizing that infrared
and visible light

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:56.720
may also be contributing
to cancer

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:58.640
and premature skin aging.

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:01.040
Sunlight comes to us
in three major bands:

00:16:01.040 --> 00:16:04.720
visible light, ultraviolet,
and infrared.

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:07.360
Visible light includes
the colors of the rainbow—

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:09.920
red, orange, yellow, green,
blue, indigo, and violet,

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:11.680
or ROYGBIV.

00:16:11.680 --> 00:16:15.680
Ultraviolet—UV—is to the right
of violet in ROYGBIV,

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:18.480
and infrared
to the left of red.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:20.240
Sunscreen can protect us

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:22.640
against the free radical damage
of UV,

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:24.400
but half
of the free radical formation

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:25.600
in our skin from the sun

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:28.000
may be from the visible
or infrared spectrums,

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:31.280
which sunscreens
may not adequately cover.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:35.040
So, that's where sun avoidance
strategies come in,

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:36.960
but you can imagine
how shade-seeking

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:38.960
could be undermined
by the solacement

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:41.680
of a sunscreen
security blanket.

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:43.520
What about vitamin D?

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:45.520
The theoretical concern
about sunscreen

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:46.880
affecting vitamin D status

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.440
does not seem to manifest
in the real world,

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:51.680
likely because
the UV dose necessary

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:54.160
for vitamin D production
is so low,

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:58.240
well before skin
turns a pinker shade.

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:00.960
However, concerns
about the systemic absorption

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:03.200
of sunscreen chemicals
were underscored

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:05.520
by the recent FDA bombshell

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:08.560
that not a single one
of sunscreen chemicals

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:10.160
in current use
can be considered

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:12.880
generally recognized as safe.

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:15.120
Only two active ingredients
got the green light,

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:18.160
the two non-chemical
"mineral" sunscreens:

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:20.640
titanium dioxide
and zinc oxide.

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:23.360
The revelation was based
on a growing body of evidence

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:26.160
that transdermal, meaning
through-the-skin, absorption

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:27.520
of sunscreen chemicals

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:30.240
was greater than
we previously thought,

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:32.960
raising unevaluated
safety concerns.

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:35.920
Unevaluated, because we
previously didn't think so much

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:37.680
got into our bloodstreams.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:39.680
In 2019, a study published

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:41.440
in the Journal of
the American Medical Association

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:43.520
found that under maximum
usage conditions

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:45.440
over a period of days,
the blood levels

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:47.440
of all the sunscreen chemicals
they tested

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:49.040
exceeded the FDA threshold

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:52.480
that could potentially waive
further safety testing.

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:56.080
Then, in 2020, they found
that just a single application

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:58.160
of all the chemical sunscreens
they tested

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:00.160
surpassed the threshold.

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:02.640
Now, just because they're
absorbed into our system,

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:04.880
doesn't necessarily mean
they're unsafe.

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:07.360
It just means they need
to be tested for safety,

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:08.880
which, the FDA determined,

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:11.280
the multibillion-dollar
sunscreen industry

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:14.160
has so far failed to do.

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:16.000
The FDA concluded
that two ingredients

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:17.120
can be considered safe—

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:19.760
zinc oxide
and titanium dioxide,

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:22.160
and two can be
considered unsafe—

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:25.520
P.A.B.A.
and trolamine salicylate.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:27.920
And the other 12 ingredients
that are currently marketed

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:30.640
have yet to be
sufficiently safety tested.

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:33.360
According to a review of more
than 700 sunscreen brands,

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:35.280
it appears P.A.B.A.
and trolamine

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:37.520
are no longer being marketed
in sunscreens

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:39.040
sold in the United States,

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:41.680
but they still may be available
in other countries.

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:43.360
Until the data are in
on the rest

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:44.560
of the chemical sunscreens,

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:48.160
I would recommend sticking
to the two mineral sunscreens.

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:51.040
Historically, they've tended
to be thicker and whiter,

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:53.680
which could lead
to even more severe underdosing,

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:55.680
but newer
micronized formulations

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:57.600
with smaller
mineral particle sizes

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:00.240
tend to be less noticeable.

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:03.120
This is particularly an issue
for those with darker skin.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:05.680
The average built-in SPF
of black skin

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:08.960
(also known in the medical
literature as "ethnic skin"

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:14.400
or "SOC," skin of color)
is about 13 naturally,

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:17.120
compared with only about three
for white skin.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:19.040
Though there haven't been
any interventional studies

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:21.920
on sunscreen effectiveness
for skin cancer prevention

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:23.760
in people with dark skin,

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:27.680
SPF 13 is not considered
sufficient sun protection;

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:30.000
so, the American Academy
of Dermatology recommends

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.640
regular sunscreen use
with an SPF of 30 or higher

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.680
for people of all skin types.

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:37.200
Unfortunately,
only about 12 percent

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:40.000
of non-Hispanic Blacks
and 31 percent of Hispanics

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:42.000
report regularly using
sunscreen,

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:45.520
compared to about 44 percent
of non-Hispanic whites.

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:47.840
Despite this,
the incidence of melanoma,

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:49.840
the deadliest skin cancer,
is five times lower

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:51.440
in Hispanics compared to whites

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:54.080
and more than 25 times lower
among Blacks.

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:57.200
However, the mortality rate
if you do get it

00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:59.200
is higher among
African Americans,

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:07.840
presumed to be due
from delayed diagnosis.

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:14.320
Okay, so in short, only two sunscreen active&nbsp;
ingredients can be considered safe at this time,

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:19.920
and that’s the two so-called mineral&nbsp;
sunscreens: titanium dioxide and zinc oxide.

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:22.920
Though remember, in the first part of the video,

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:26.640
we talked about how that may really only cover UV—

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:33.360
the ultraviolet range—so sun&nbsp;
avoidance is still important,

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:42.880
since maybe half the free radicals created by&nbsp;
sun exposure are from the non-covered spectrum.

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:53.760
And so, so it’s—so avoidance, in addition to&nbsp;
using good sunscreens, is still important.

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:58.080
Absolutely outrageous that the safety&nbsp;
testing wasn’t done by industry,

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:03.040
but now they’re going to be&nbsp;
forced to—and it’s about time.

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:05.520
All right, Q&amp;A time.

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:07.760
Happy to talk about anything that came up today

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:11.920
or any related topics on skin aging in general.

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:15.840
Got a whole chapter on it in How Not to Age,

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:23.520
and so we could bring up the pre-release sneak&nbsp;
preview and go through anything you’d like.

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:27.440
Let me just give NutritionFacts staff&nbsp;
five minutes to compile the questions

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:30.320
that have been submitted so&nbsp;
far through the Q&amp;A button.

00:21:30.320 --> 00:21:32.480
It’s not too late if you want to ask one now—

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:36.069
and then I will be right back.

00:21:36.069 --> 00:56:10.060
[Q&amp;A portion not captioned]

